MaizeMeals ingredient-flag datasets
Retrieved: 2026-11-18

These are compact derived files for scanning University of Michigan NetNutrition
ingredient paragraphs. The raw taxonomies are not in this repository.

1. additives.json
   Open Food Facts additives taxonomy (ODbL).
   JSON export: https://static.openfoodfacts.org/data/taxonomies/additives.json
   English synonyms: https://github.com/openfoodfacts/openfoodfacts-server/blob/main/taxonomies/additives.txt
   The JSON export keeps one English display name per additive. Phrase-match
   names come from the English synonym lines in the taxonomy source. Names are
   lowercased, punctuation-folded, and limited to English. Very generic tokens
   (salt, water, sugar, oil, starch, spice, natural flavor, and similar) are
   omitted so they do not match ordinary dining-label words.
   US/UK spelling pairs that were generated from those names, not added as new
   chemicals: aluminium/aluminum, sulphate/sulfate, sulphite/sulfite, colour/color.

2. ingredient-watch.json
   Curated MaizeMeals watch list of seed oils and palm oil. The moderate
   concern is a watch-list label. It is not an Open Food Facts additive and
   not a toxicity determination.
   Oil names were checked against the Open Food Facts ingredients taxonomy
   (ODbL): https://static.openfoodfacts.org/data/taxonomies/ingredients.json
   That file lists most of these oils and was not vendored. It does not supply
   the moderate concern. maize oil and the spelling grapeseed oil are
   dining-label synonyms.

Concern values are copied from structured taxonomy fields, or marked unknown.
They are not Yuka grades and not an invented score.

On each additive, concern is taken from these fields when they are present:
  efsa_evaluation_overexposure_risk
    en:high -> high
    en:moderate -> moderate
    en:no -> low
  If that field is absent:
    efsa_evaluation_exposure_mean_greater_than_adi
    efsa_evaluation_exposure_95th_greater_than_adi
    efsa_evaluation_exposure_mean_greater_than_noael
    efsa_evaluation_exposure_95th_greater_than_noael
    A value that names a population group (anything other than en:no-group)
    means exposure above the reference -> high.
    en:no-group means no population group was above the reference and is not
    treated as exposure above ADI. If every present exposure field is
    en:no-group, concern is low.
  If none of these fields are present, concern is unknown, even when the
  taxonomy links an EFSA opinion title (efsa_evaluation) and even when the
  class is colour or preservative. The class is still recorded, and colour,
  preservative, sweetener, and flavour-enhancer also set a flag.

When a child additive has none of those EFSA fields, the same fields are read
from the nearest parent with the same numeric E-number. concern_reasons then
name that parent id. The child's own field always wins when it exists.

Class flags: colour/color -> food-coloring, plus preservative, sweetener, and
flavour-enhancer. EFSA flags record the structured values above:
efsa-overexposure-high, efsa-overexposure-moderate, efsa-overexposure-no,
exposure-above-adi, exposure-above-noael.

The seed-oil list is a curated watch list.

3. Open Food Facts API v3
   Spec checked 2026-11-18:
   https://github.com/openfoodfacts/openfoodfacts-server/blob/main/docs/api/ref/api-v3.yaml
   The file says API v3.4 and that v3 is under development. It does not
   replace additives.json for a NetNutrition ingredient sentence, which has
   no barcode. Read requests need no auth. A User-Agent is recommended.

   GET /api/v3/product/{code}
     Path code is the barcode. Query fields selects the response (none, raw,
     all, or a comma-separated list). knowledge_panels is excluded from all
     unless requested. The product object is schema product_v3, which pulls
     in Product-Ingredients and Product-Nutrition-v3.5. Those hold packaged
     product ingredients, additives, allergens, and nutrition. This call
     cannot scan a dining-hall sentence.

   PATCH /api/v3/product/{code}
     code may be the barcode, or test to analyze a payload without saving.
     The body product object can include language-specific fields such as
     ingredients text. The spec says this write API is still changing. It is
     not a bulk concern list.

   GET /api/v3/taxonomy_canonicalize_tags
     Query tagtype (example ingredients), local_tags_list (comma-separated
     tags), optional lc. Returns canonical_tags_list, for example
     en:sugar,en:water. This canonicalizes tags. It does not parse a
     parenthetical paragraph and it does not return EFSA overexposure.

   GET /api/v3/taxonomy_display_tags
     Query tagtype, canonical_tags_list, optional lc. Returns display_tags.

   GET /api/v3/taxonomy_suggestions
     Query tagtype, optional string (alias term), lc, cc, categories, shape,
     limit (default 25, max 400), get_synonyms. Returns suggestions and,
     when get_synonyms is 1, matched_synonyms. Autocomplete only.

   GET /api/v3/tag/{tagtype}/{tag_or_tagid}
     Returns knowledge_panels for one tag. The spec lists category packaging
     stats, related categories, and an optional unknown-NOVA panel. It does
     not list additive risk panels.

   GET /api/v3.4/attribute_groups
     Returns attribute_groups used for personal preferences. Each attribute
     has id, name, values, and optional parameters. Unwanted ingredients use
     parameter id attribute_unwanted_ingredients_tags, tagtype ingredients,
     type tags. These are user settings, not additive grades.
